<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sulawesi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sulawesi is a large island in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. It was linked to the trading networks of the archipelago. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire</a> list it among the islands connected by Majapahit's maritime trade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sulawesi.jpg" alt="Sulawesi" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeff Schmaltz, MODIS Land Rapid Response Team, NASA GSFC · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fires_on_Sulawesi,_Indonesia_(MODIS_2015-10-22).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sulawesi, also known as Celebes, is one of the four Greater Sunda Islands and the world's 11th-largest island, with an area of 186,064 km2. It lies east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> and west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Maluku Islands</a>. Its landmass has four peninsulas, separated by the gulfs of Tomini, Tolo and Boni. The interior is rugged and mostly non-volcanic, so the peninsulas have traditionally been remote from each other, with better connections by sea than by road. The name Celebes was given by Portuguese explorers and first recorded in 1512. Sulawesi is part of Wallacea, and many of its animals and plants are found nowhere else.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The oldest evidence of humans on Sulawesi is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a> made by archaic humans, dated to at least 1.04 million years ago, found at the Calio site in the south-west. Cave paintings at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mures-river">Maros</a> were announced in 2014 to be about 40,000 years old; a hand stencil is 39,900 years old, and a pig in Pettakere Cave has a minimum age of 35,400 years. From the 13th century, access to prestige trade goods and to sources of iron began to change older cultural patterns and allowed ambitious individuals to build larger political units.</p><p>The first Europeans to visit were the Portuguese Simão de Abreu in 1523 and Gomes de Sequeira in 1525, sent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Moluccas</a> in search of gold. A Portuguese base in Makassar lasted until 1665, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> took it. The Dutch had arrived in 1605 and were followed by the English. From 1660 the Dutch fought Gowa, the main Makassar power, and in 1669 Admiral Speelman forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-hasanuddin">Sultan Hasanuddin</a> to sign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bongaya">Treaty of Bongaya</a>, which gave trade to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arung-palakka">Arung Palakka</a> of Bone aided the Dutch and became regional overlord.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Luwu was the first kingdom to adopt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, in February 1605, and Gowa-Talloq followed in September; the Gorontalo and Mongondow peoples of the north largely converted in the 19th century. Islam is the majority religion. The island was one province between 1945 and 1960 and is now divided into six provinces: Gorontalo, West Sulawesi, South Sulawesi, Central Sulawesi, Southeast Sulawesi and North Sulawesi. The 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 19,896,951 people, and Makassar is the largest city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sulawesi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Expeditions against Bali in 1846, 1848 and 1849, the Banjarmasin War from 1859 and campaigns in <b>Sulawesi</b> and Borneo extended Dutch power step by step.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>About 87 per cent of Indonesians are Muslims, making it the largest Muslim-majority country in the world, but the state officially recognises six religions, and Bali remains largely Hindu while parts of Sumatra, <b>Sulawesi</b>, eastern Indonesia and Papua are Christian.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Maritime trade was central to the empire's wealth, linking ports from Sumatra and Borneo to <b>Sulawesi</b> and Maluku with international networks reaching China, India and the Malay Peninsula.</blockquote><a href="/empire/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…met by slave raiding: Iranun and Balangingi Sama fleets based in the Sulu zone seized, by his estimates, thousands of captives a year from the Visayas, Luzon, the Moluccas, <b>Sulawesi</b> and the Malay world, many of whom were set to fishing and collecting marine produce or sold onward.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sulu-sultanate">Sulu Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>They came when Australia was part of Sahul, joined to New Guinea by land, but still had to cross the sea beyond the Wallace Line, perhaps island-hopping from <b>Sulawesi</b> or by way of Timor.</blockquote><a href="/culture/aboriginal-australians">Aboriginal Australians · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Recent DNA testing of the remains of ancient Chamorros, however, showed that the lineage of both the Unai and the Latte periods began in eastern Indonesia, most likely <b>Sulawesi</b>, with no direct prehistoric connection to the Philippines.</blockquote><a href="/culture/chamorro-people">Chamorro people · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
